WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.027
What embeddings miss, and how to check.

2
00:00:02.307 --> 00:00:08.344
A single vector per passage compresses a lot of text into a few kilobytes, and something has to give.

3
00:00:08.624 --> 00:00:20.165
Be suspicious of how well an embedding represents exact identifiers (error codes, SKUs, ticket numbers), rare names, numbers, and fine logical distinctions such as negation.

4
00:00:20.445 --> 00:00:34.803
Don’t assume either way: test pairs from your own data, such as “error E-417” against the article about E-417 and the article about E-416, and see whether the scores separate them.

5
00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:37.259
The research gives a reason for caution.

6
00:00:37.539 --> 00:00:54.861
The BEIR benchmark (Thakur and colleagues, 2021) evaluated retrieval systems on 18 datasets from domains they weren’t trained on, and found BM25 keyword search was a robust baseline that dense retrievers often failed to beat out of domain.

7
00:00:55.141 --> 00:01:03.461
Models have improved a lot since, but the lesson stands: semantic and keyword search fail differently, which is why week 3 combines them.

8
00:01:03.741 --> 00:01:09.629
The checks and balances for this lesson are simple and worth doing every time you touch embeddings.

9
00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:15.541
Look at actual nearest neighbors for a handful of real queries, not just aggregate scores.

10
00:01:15.821 --> 00:01:20.131
Confirm vector norms (if the docs say unit length, assert it in a test).

11
00:01:20.411 --> 00:01:25.061
And confirm the metric your vector store uses matches the one your model expects.

12
00:01:25.341 --> 00:01:29.203
Lesson 2 turns “look at neighbors” into a measured evaluation.
